  "query_preview": "..\nFriday through Sunday: The Inland NW will remain in a somewhat \nactive pattern, with periodic shower and thunderstorm chances \nand temperatures a bit closer to seasonal norms. Highs will be \nin the upper 70s to 80s Friday, then will warm slightly toward \nthe mid 80s through mid 90s this weekend. A baggy upper trough \nretrogrades west, but impulses and lingering moisture (with \nPWATs around 120-160% of normal) will work with daily daytime \ninstability to bring continued precipitation chances. The focus\nfor precipitation potential will be around the mountains most \ndays. Friday the best potential will be in the Cascades and \nsecondarily over the eastern mountains and near the far \nsoutheast CWA. A similar focus is forecast on Saturday, but \noverall the risk will be lower as that trough retrogrades off \nthe coast. Even smaller chances will be found Sunday, found in \nspotty fashion around the Cascades and northern mountains and \npotentially a bit more coverage near the far southeast CWA, \nespecially later in the day as a wave surges along the boundary \nstalled in that region. \n\nAs for fire weather and lightning and wind threat, a modest \nchance for thunderstorms will be found near the Cascades again \nFriday afternoon and evening; elevated fire weather conditions \nare forecast but there is a better chance for thunderstorms to\nbe wet which will limit the fire threat. Winds will generally be\nout of the north to northeast, with some diurnal terrain \ninfluences. A little breezy down the Purcell Trench and over the\nColumbia Basin Friday morning, with gusts of 15-20 mph possible\nbefore slackening Friday afternoon.\n\nMonday to Thursday: The offshore trough starts to push back into \nthe region Monday into Tuesday, but models start to diverge with \nwhat to do with it later Tuesday into Thursday with some slower \nto progress it east and others shifting it through more rapidly.\nYet either way models tend to start to bring some drier air \ninto the region after Monday. Expect a broader shower threat on \nMonday, relatively speaking, meaning around the mountains and \nthe eastern third of WA. However the overall best threat will be\naround the Idaho Panhandle. With that said, the potential for \nmuch precipitation to come out of these is low. A chance for \nt-storms will found Monday afternoon and evening too. For \nTuesday to Thursday the main shower chances retreat to the \nmountains and look more isolated in coverage. Temperatures will\nbe a more seasonal in the 80s to low 90s. Wind will be a bit \nbreezy Monday and Tuesday, especially near the Cascade gaps and \nColumbia Basin. Right now winds are not expected to be strong \nenough to warrant much of a fire weather concern, as winds are \ngenerally less than 15 mph and gusts rise to near 15-20 mph. \n/Solveig",
